  "reading_order_details": "Initial source read: necessary ending of 4.7, complete 4.8, and 5 introduction/5.1–5.6 preview. Live root→chapter-04→4.8 reread, then complete chapter 05→06→07 in consecutive batches, then 08 introduction and 8.1. Complete source warmup and targeted live earlier sections followed. T.4 was fully reread in two source chunks; original interface code was read next and T.4/prerequisite/boundary navigation was actually clicked. Later navigation revisits did not replace these full readings.",
  "summary": "This path is coherent for a mathematically prepared high-school/basic-college reader using the linked Python/tensor refreshers. Chapter 4’s assembled score/gradient interface becomes chapter 5’s actual update loop and defensible held-out measurement. Chapter 6 then makes the counted token and ID contract explicit, including common original-byte comparison and structural IDs. Chapter 7 retains the next-token model while changing serialization and supervised targets, separates loss masking from reading permissions, and carries the same split/budget rules into answer, corruption, forgetting, and replay comparisons. Its posttraining overview and chapter 8’s first rubric continue separating task correctness from expression. I found no blocking source continuity issue in the complete assigned path.",
  "checks": {
    "prerequisites": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "The 4.7 alignment/backward interface and 1.12/1.13 update/gradient-clear prerequisites precede 5.1. The warmup covers Python roles/collections, tensor axes, logs, gradients, and paths. 6.1 explains code points/bytes before ByteLevel BPE, 6.6 explains dedicated control IDs before 7.1 roles, and 6.8’s one-shift/window contract precedes truncation. 7.3–7.7 explicitly point back to chain gradients, embedding, positions, and causal permission; those current sections were personally read. The initial AdamW use is locally scoped as a ready-made updater with details promised and supplied in 5.4/5.5. Future posttraining methods are introduced as optional routes rather than required prior knowledge."
    },
    "terminology": {
      "status": "pass",
      "details": "ID, sequence position, physical padded index, semantic position ID, feature width, vocabulary size, and valid-target count retain distinct meanings. Loss is the supplied target’s negative natural-log probability, not error count or answer truth. Data normalization is explicitly distinguished from LN; momentum v versus Adam squared-gradient v are locally redefined. PAD is a legal input ID and -100 a target ignore value. Temperature’s positive-number formula and this project’s temperature=0 greedy convention are separately stated. SFT changes training targets, while role prompts alone do not update weights; RLHF is a feedback source/process and PPO an update method."
    },
    "examples_and_exercises": {
